    "result_text": "All wired correctly. Here's a summary of what I implemented for **version 2 of the owner identity**.\n\n## Design\n\nThe lever that kept structural impact low was redefining a single method, `OwnerLocality.region(owner)`, to return the **plain** region code (from postcode/city) instead of reading it back off the memberId. That decoupled every user-facing consumer (`locality`, `timezone`, `ownerSegment`) from the identifiers in one edit \u2014 so mixing a `V2` tag into the memberId's region code couldn't leak into those fields.\n\n## Changes\n\n**Identity derivation (V2 tag mixed into every identifier):**\n- `OwnerLocality` \u2014 `region()` now returns the plain region; new `regionV2()` appends the fixed `\"V2\"` tag to the region code used inside the memberId.\n- `AssignCustomerCode` \u2014 memberId now embeds `regionV2(...)` (e.g. `NSWV2\u2026`).\n- `AssignHousehold` \u2014 householdId hash input gets `|V2` mixed in.\n- `OwnerIdentity` \u2014 identityKey hash input gets `|V2` mixed in.\n\nEvery identifier changes; no V1 value can recur. `locality` stays `NSW`, `timezone` and the owner segment's derived region stay plain (segment recomputes to `STANDARD_METRO`).\n\n**Response shape:**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 new `OwnerIdentity` schema; `Owner` gains top-level integer `apiVersion` and nested `identity`; removed the three top-level identifier fields.\n- `OwnerMapper` \u2014 `apiVersion=2`, a small `toOwnerIdentity(owner)` helper groups the three identifiers; dropped the old top-level mappings.\n\n**Audit schema v2:**\n- `EmitOwnerCreatedEvent` \u2014 event now carries `schemaVersion:2` and the owner segment recomputed from the V2 identity.\n\nRequest fields are untouched. `AcceptanceTest` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`), and the audit line confirms the runtime output: `{\"schemaVersion\":2,...,\"memberId\":\"NSWV2...\",\"ownerSegment\":\"STANDARD_METRO\",...}`.",
